course: dictionary
lesson: 13432
title: Words that sound the same
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound exactly the same but mean different things. Writers call these homophonous words. The word homophonous describes the pair or group, and the words often have different spellings too.
explain: «I ate a pear at lunch.» and «I bought a pair of socks.» Both words sound the same when you say them aloud. Pear is a fruit, but pair means two things that go together. The rest of the sentence tells you which one is meant.
explain: This is why a spell checker cannot catch every mistake. «Their dog is wet» and «There is a dog outside» use real words, but they sound the same, so you must check the meaning, not just the sound.
fact: Homophonous means sounding exactly the same but having different meanings.
fact: Homophonous words often have different spellings, such as «pair» and «pear».
fact: «There», «their» and «they're» are homophonous: «There is a cat», «Their cat is black», «They're going home» means they are going home.
fact: «To», «too» and «two» are homophonous: «I want to go too», «I have two pets».
fact: «Knight» and «night» are homophonous: «The knight rode out at night.» In music, homophonous means one main tune with the other parts moving along as chords, but this meaning is rare.
exercise: What does homophonous mean? | *Sounding exactly the same but meaning something different | Spelled the same but sounding different | Written in capital letters | Very long and hard to say
exercise: «I ate a pear at lunch.» Which word sounds the same as pear but means two things that go together? | Pear | Plum | *Pair | Pizza
exercise: Ella writes «___ going to the park.» Which word fits the gap? | Thair | Their | There | *They're
exercise: The knight rode out at night. Which word sounds exactly like night? | Nite spelled wrong | *Knight | Nine | Nest
test: «The knight rode out at night.» Which word sounds exactly the same as night? | Nice | Nest | *Knight | Nine
test: Which two words are homophonous? | Sun and moon | Cat and dog | Red and blue | *Pair and pear
exam: Ella writes «Their going to the shop.» Which word should replace Their? | Them | Thee | *They're | Theirs
